item.content:encoded:
Below is a post I wrote about the FLX1s before it broke at the start of September, there was a problem with the display controller and it would randomly go into a messed up mode that wasn’t readable but also changed a bit in relation to button presses etc so the phone was still working. Also I could ssh to the phone when it was in that state if it was on my home wifi.It took almost 2 months to get a replacement phone but I now have that working and the new 14.1.0 OS release. So some of the below may be obsolete when it comes to the latest release but I decided to publish it anyway for history if nothing else.One thing I had known about but not mentioned before is that the icon in the drop-down menu for the hotspot will often be gray (indicating hotspot-off) when it’s actually on. To address that I have to click on it (which then indicates that the hotspot is on) and then click on it again to turn it off.I have given up trying to get Tokodon working on the phone for the moment. Tokodon is a nice Mastodon client that would allow me to get notifications from Mastodon.Apparently the Furilabs people are working on some issues related to this.Bluetooth is currently a bit unreliable. When I turn on my car (Nissan LEAF) the phone usually either doesn’t connect or claims to be connected while not sending audio to the car. When it does connect my favourite music player (Lollypop) usually doesn’t display the album and track names. I’ve found that turning my car off and on again usually makes it connect properly.I admit the possibility of my car not doing things correctly in this regard. But every Android phone I tried just worked, they would connect as soon as the car was turned on and correctly display track names. So if the car is doing things wrong and needs a software work around then Linux is at fault for not doing that – we can’t get tens of millions of cars and hundreds of millions of other weird devices fixed!I also note that my Debian laptop and a friend’s Debian laptop also have problems connecting to my car. So it’s quite possible that Debian code is at fault (the FLX1s is running a modified version of Debian).It currently appears to have no support for handset mode so I can’t answer phone calls through the car interface. But this is a lesser problem.I plan to do more testing with Debian laptops and file some bug reports if I can find out enough useful information. My current knowledge of this is insufficient to create a useful bug report. I do have quite a few bluetooth headsets and test laptops so I plan to start by testing Debian and Ubuntu on all the combinations – last time I tested Ubuntu 24.04 on Dell laptops worked much better than Debian/Trixie on Thinkpads.After a set of updates including the following Wifi hotspot seems to be working.adaptation-furiphone-radon (1.3.0+git20260513192743.e94f2ec.forky.production) forky; urgency=medium

  * NetworkManager: sync hotspot channel/band with STA and restart on change
  * debian: depend on libhybris-utils and libgbinder-tools
  * startup: drop schedtune and add m4u debug log level
  * startup: enable glove and disable edge palm
  * device: drop flashlightd-scalable signatures

 -- Bardia Moshiri <bardia@furilabs.com>  Wed, 13 May 2026 15:27:43 -0400Connecting the power cable now makes the phone immediately make the click sound and it starts charging while previously there was a 22 second delay. Once I get used to this I can avoid the problem of failing to plug it in firmly before going to bed and having it run flat overnight.The following command will send a message via SMS.dbus-send --system --print-reply --dest=org.ofono /ril_0 org.ofono.MessageManager.SendMessage string:"+61400000000" string:"this is the message"Also calls can be scripted via the following command:xdg-open tel://+61400000000My aim is to get contacts synchronised via my own cloud service. Nextcloud is supposed to be able to do this. It’s been years since I first tried this, my most recent serious attempt at Nextcloud was in 2021 [4] and I couldn’t get the Android app for NextCloud to connect. Since then I have made more recent attempts and been unable to get the Android or Linux desktop clients to connect to it.A friend gave me a test account on his Nextcloud server which allowed me to connect the Linux desktop app, his hypothesis was that it was somehow related to using Debian and that his server worked because it was on Ubuntu. I have since installed my own Nextcloud test server on the Ubuntu variant used by the Oracle cloud VMs which had the same apparent problem. I conclude that it’s not Debian at fault but something related to how I configured it.In the meantime after a couple of months of having no named contacts I exported the contacts from my Android phone as a VCF file and imported them into the Gnome contacts program. I had to run “” for it to get the names for the contacts, but the phone call program found them without needing to do that.A missing feature of Chatty (the default SMS and Jabber program for Phosh/Phoc systems) is archiving old messages. I use Chatty to receive Jabber messages from my monitoring system which can be a lot of messages. The following shell code deletes all Jabber messages older than 2 days. Note that this relies on “” matching  Jabber messages which I’m not sure is going to be the case on all systems or all versions of Chatty.for ID in $(echo "select id from users where type=3;" | sqlite3 ~/.purple/chatty/db/chatty-history.db); do
  echo "delete from messages where sender_id=$ID and time < unixepoch('now') - (48 * 3600);" | sqlite3 ~/.purple/chatty/db/chatty-history.db
doneThe following shell code extracts a dump of all messages from Chatty.echo "select messages.id,users.username,users.alias,messages.body from messages inner join users on messages.sender_id=users.id;" |sqlite3 ~/.purple/chatty/db/chatty-history.dbAn update to the Chatty program for SMS and Matrix has made it crash repeatedly. There is a request in the list for Chatty upstream to have a systemd-user unit to restart it which when implemented will reduce the scope of the problem (but not solve it). I've setup a monitoring system that uses  to notify me when Chatty isn't running or when 4G data turns off (a recurring problem). This crashing is allegedly due to Jabber so I installed Dino-IM for Jabber and have for the moment at least stopped using Chatty for Jabber.It turned out that having Chatty not do Jabber didn't stop it crashing. However having alerts separated from SMS can be convenient and also my long term plan is to use a phone with Plasma Mobile and Spacebar (the SMS program for Plasma Mobile) doesn't do Jabber. So I will continue using Dino-IM.echo "delete from message where time < unixepoch('now') - (48 * 3600);" | sqlite3 ~/.local/share/dino/dino.dbI put in the following cron job to stop this log file from using all the storage space:1 0 * * *	echo -n > /home/furios/.local/share/andromeda/data/system/uiderrors.txtI gave up on attempts to force the Wifi configuration to have a fixed MAC that I could use for a fixed IPv4 address so I manually configured the address.I've had problems with the reliability of the 4G service, sometimes it stop working and I need to stop and restart it. I setup my etbe-mon monitoring system on the phone [5] and configured it to ssh to localhost and run  to send a local notification. This means I get notified if my phone loses net access and can't access regular monitoring. Then to monitor the 4G connection (which I want to have working even when Wifi is connected) I had the following monitor line:monitor ping.monitor -I ccmni1 -c 5 8.8.8.8 ;;That uses the  interface for pinging 8.8.8.8 (Google DNS). That worked for a while but I discovered that sometimes the interface for 4G would change to , so I put the following monitor script under /usr/local/mon.d:#!/bin/bash
DEV=$(route -n|grep ^0.0.0.0.*ccm |sed -e s/^.*ccmn/ccmn/)
exec /usr/lib/mon/mon.d/ping.monitor -I $DEV -c 5 8.8.8.8I couldn't get Tokodon to work, not the native package, not running in a Debian/Testing container, and not running a Flatpak. It seems that this is all graphics library issues. So I wrote a Perl program to run "toot notifications &-json" and then send the relevant parts of the output via XMPP. As I already use XMPP for server monitoring there's no extra effort to have an extra account and use it for notifications about Mastodon activity. The Perl script is at the bottom of the post and won't be in the RSS feed.At some future time I may change the script to send the notifications by Matrix instead to have all my social media stuff in the same place.#!/usr/bin/perl
# depends on Debian packages: toot libjson-pp-perl go-sendxmpp
use strict;
use JSON::PP;

my $homeurl = "https://mastodon.nzoss.nz/";
my $lastfile = "mast/last_id";
my $xaddr = "russell\@coker.com.au";

my $jsontxt;
while()
{
  $jsontxt .= $_;
}

my $data = decode_json($jsontxt);

my $last_id = 0, my $new_last_id;
if(open(ID, "<$lastfile"))
{
  $last_id = ;
  close(ID);
}
$new_last_id = $last_id;

for(my $i = 0; $data->[$i]; $i++)
{
  my $id = $data->[$i]{id};
  if($id <= $last_id)
  {
    next;
  }
  if($id > $new_last_id)
  {
    $new_last_id = $id;
  }
  open(XMPP, "|go-sendxmpp $xaddr") or die "Can't send XMPP";
  my $type = $data->[$i]{type};
  print XMPP "type: $type\n";
  print XMPP "their_url: $homeurl\@$data->[$i]{account}{acct}\n";
  print XMPP "display_name: $data->[$i]{account}{display_name}\n";
  if($type ne "follow")
  {
    print XMPP "status_url: $data->[$i]{status}{url}\n";
    print XMPP "content: $data->[$i]{status}{content}\n";
  }
  print XMPP "created_at: $data->[$i]{created_at}\n";
  print XMPP "\n";
  close(XMPP);
}

open(ID, ">$lastfile") or die "Can't write to $lastfile";
print ID $new_last_id;
close(ID);
